# I2 sizing — walker-mounted cable pelvic interface (slack / nudge / arrest)

Status: preliminary statics + simulation feasibility analysis, 22 Oct 2026. Not human-tested, not
hardware-tested, not safety-certified; it supports no fall-arrest claim.

Rounding policy (generated file: do not edit by hand; see `make_report.py`, `publish.py`, `templates/`). Every number below is
filled from the JSON in `data/` by `publish.py`, so a published bound is never better than its source value: loads, tensions,
torques, required ratings, resultants, maxima, upper CI bounds and required mass are rounded UP; capacities, safety factors,
friction capacity, slide limits, envelopes, minima, coverage percentages and lower CI bounds are rounded DOWN; a confidence
interval has its lower bound DOWN and its upper bound UP at 1 decimal. Only descriptive statistics of the simulated sample (the
"median" and "p95" figures) and nominal geometry are rounded to nearest, and they are always labelled as such. Model inputs
and assumptions (for example the 60 kg baseline walker, 15 kN/m cable stiffness, 85 kg patient) are stated, not computed.
Interpolated quantities are linear between the two adjacent tabulated JSON points.

D3 geometry has ONE source of truth: `data/d3_contract.json`. `sizing.py`, `run_analysis.py`, `slip_analysis.py` and
`cad/build_cad_d3.py` all read it; `test_d3_consistency.py` checks the generated CAD parts against it (1 mm).

Reproduce: `.venv/Scripts/python analysis/i2_sizing/instrument_sim.py [tau_lock]` (0.28, 0.15, 0.08),
then `run_analysis.py`, `slip_analysis.py`, `fbd.py`, `make_report.py` (regenerates this file, `results/sizing_D3_summary.md` and the
derived fields of the contract), and `cad/build_cad_d3.py` for the CAD.
Tests: `pytest analysis/i2_sizing` (`test_sizing.py`, `test_d3_consistency.py`, `test_publish.py`).

## 1. Bottom line
1. The {f491} N in the concept review is `Dc·v` of the SPEC catch spring-damper (`F = -Kc(x-xlock) - Dc v`,
   Dc = 800 N·s/m), not a physical arrest requirement. The force needed to stop the simulated patients within
   0.25 m is much lower (§3): median {desc:R/loads/280/Freq/median:0} N, p95 {desc:R/loads/280/Freq/p95:0} N (descriptive statistics of the sample, nearest).
2. As proposed (60 kg walker, horizontal cables at pelvis height, no outriggers) I2 tips statically at
   {cap:R/envelope_sf1/D0 as proposed/min:0} N (SF 1) and has SF {d0_sf_491} at {f491} N.
3. Feasible only with five changes together: a brake that slips at a set force (force limiter);
   mixed-height anchors (corners high on the {geo:K/corner_anchors/0/2:2} m columns, the rest at pelvis height); an {int:K/mass} kg walker
   with ~{ballast} kg low ballast (CG z {geo:K/cg/2:2} m); outrigger feet deployed on catch (+{geo:K/outrigger_ex:2} m all round); the BWS
   anchor above the pelvis (x = {geo:K/bws_anchor/0:2} m), not on the rear beam.
4. Force wording (used everywhere): the group-tuned passive slip brakes give ≥ {int:K/F_lim} N along the fall direction
   ({cap:S/group_tuned/Fpar_min:0} N minimum over {int:S/group_tuned/n_directions} directions) and ≤ {int:K/resultant_bound_all_slip} N ({req:S/group_tuned/Fh_max:1} N) horizontal resultant (the perpendicular component is up to {req:S/group_tuned/perp_max:0} N;
   the resultant deviates up to {req:S/group_tuned/ang_max:0} deg from the fall direction). The resultant stays below the {int:K/envelope_SF1p5} N static envelope
   of D3 (SF {desc:R/params/SF:1}; SF 1.0 to {cap:R/envelope_sf1/D3 recommended/min:0} N). Tip SF ≥ {cap:S/group_tuned/sf_min:2} and sliding SF ≥ {cap:S/group_tuned/sf_slide_min:2} at the full slip resultant (friction
   capacity ≥ {fcap_min} N at μ = {geo:K/mu_pad:1} in the {int:K/F_lim} N cases). Pelvis lift ≤ {req:S/group_tuned/lift_max:0} N under passive slip (cap {desc:R/params/XI_ARREST:1} BW = {cap:R/params/CAP_ARREST:0} N for 60 kg).
5. Denominator, stated explicitly (HEADLINE, passive-slip metric): among the {ncaught} trials that the CP m = 0.03
   simulation ({ntot} trials, lock {lock28} s) already ended as CAUGHT, the group-tuned passive slip brakes on D3 (corner {int:K/T_slip/corner},
   lateral {int:K/T_slip/lateral}, centre {int:K/T_slip/centre} N; for each trial's fall direction they deliver {cap:R/dyn/passive/280/Fpar_min:1}-{req:R/dyn/passive/280/Fpar/max:1} N along the fall, median {desc:R/dyn/passive/280/Fpar/median:0} N (descriptive), plus
   up to {req:S/group_tuned/perp_max:0} N perpendicular) arrest {covp:280} within 0.25 m (1-D stop with the
   along-fall force), with static tip SF >= {desc:R/dyn/passive_params/SF:1} and no sliding > {slide_tol_cm} cm under the FULL resultant, and no dynamic tip.
   Comparison, the earlier metric (fixed {int:K/F_lim} N along the fall, QP-distributed tensions): {covf:280}. The other {never}/{ntot} trials never engage the catch, so I2 does not change them. This is NOT 99 % of
   all trials, not a population guarantee, and not a fall reduction: the {falls0} baseline falls ({falls0_pct} %) are unchanged
   and the {nfail} non-arrested caught trials are potential additional falls, {falls0} + {nfail} = {worst_falls}/{ntot} = {worst_pct} % at worst
   (counts, under the simplifications of section 2.6). An empirical, conditional result of one seeded simulation.
6. Why a {int:K/F_lim} N along-fall target and not the {int:K/envelope_SF1p5} N static (SF {desc:R/params/SF:1}) envelope: coverage plateaus above ~{int:K/F_lim} N
   (fixed-force metric: {int:R/dyn/curves/280|D3 recommended/F_lim=300/k}/{ncaught} at {int:K/F_lim} N, {int:R/dyn/curves/280|D3 recommended/F_lim=400/k}/{ncaught} at 400 N); the ~{margin_420} N between the ≤ {int:K/resultant_bound_all_slip} N passive resultant and {int:K/envelope_SF1p5} N,
   and the headroom of the envelope itself, are held as margin for (a) the spread of the per-spool slip resultant
   (section 4b), (b) friction uncertainty (D3 slides at {cap:R/slide_limit/mu=0.3/F_slide:0} N if the pads give mu = 0.3), (c) dynamic overshoot and
   incomplete outrigger deployment (without outriggers the envelope is {s_noout} N). Lock-delay sensitivity, D3 caught
   trials, passive-slip metric (fixed {int:K/F_lim} N metric in brackets): {lock28} s {covps:280} ({covfs:280});
   0.15 s {covps:150} ({covfs:150}); 0.08 s {covps:080} ({covfs:080}) (a faster lock means a
   faster COM at engagement and a higher load).
7. Timing: with the SPEC {lock28} s lock the catch engages at {lock28} s after decision and the COM stops at
   ≈{t_stop_med_abs} s (median, descriptive; p95 {t_stop_p95_abs} s, rounded up; passive-slip model), inside the 0.3–0.5 s window. A faster lock (assumed ~0.14 s) is
   plausible but undemonstrated, and it raises the load (§3).

## 2. Model and assumptions
Frame per SPEC §4: x forward, y left, z up. D3 geometry is read from `data/d3_contract.json` (the CAD reads the
same file): footprint {geo:K/footprint_xy/0:2} × {geo:K/footprint_xy/1:2} m; wheel contacts inset by the wheel radius {geo:K/wheel_radius:3} (x = {geo:K/wheels_x/0:3}/{geo:K/wheels_x/1:3}, y = ±{geo:K/wheels_y:3}).
Columns {geo:K/corner_anchors/0/2:2} m at x = {geo:K/corner_anchors/0/0:2}/{geo:K/corner_anchors/2/0:2}, y = ±{geo:K/corner_anchors/0/1:2}. Nominal pelvis P = ({geo:K/pelvis/0:2}, 0, {geo:K/pelvis/2:2}); belt (D3) half-depth {geo:K/belt/hd:2},
half-width {geo:K/belt/hw:3} (the CAD value, needed for the mannequin to fit; the SPEC value 0.11/0.19 is kept only for D0-D2).
Each anchor is the cable exit point: the CAD places every spool housing so that its fairlead tip lies exactly on the
contract anchor (checked at 1 mm).

2.1 Cables: u_i from belt attachment (ray from the pelvis centre toward the anchor, intersected with the belt ellipse)
to anchor; pelvis force F_p = Σ T_i u_i; frame force at anchor −T_i u_i.
QP: min Σ T² s.t. Σ T_i u_i,xy = F_des, T_min = 2 N ≤ T ≤ T_max (1500 N arrest, 250 N nudge); solved by
bounded least squares + null-space polish (residual < 1e-9 N). Pelvis lift capped ({desc:R/params/XI_NUDGE:2} BW nudge,
{desc:R/params/XI_ARREST:1} BW arrest, lightest patient 60 kg). Layouts: 4 corner anchors; 6 adds lateral ({geo:K/lateral_anchors/0/0:2}, ±{geo:K/lateral_anchors/0/1:2});
8 adds rear-centre and front-centre; optional overhead BWS cable. Cables only pull → forces toward the
open front of the U need front anchors (open routing question).

2.2 Walker statics: weight at CG, cable reactions at anchors, ground reactions at 4 wheels (+4 outrigger
feet when deployed). Tipping when the ground CoP leaves the support polygon; tip SF = multiple of cable
loads that puts the CoP on an edge; sliding SF = μ(N0 + λN1)/(λ|H|). D0/D1 walker 60 kg, CG
(0.40, 0, 0.45); μ = {geo:K/mu_pad:1} assumed. Patient weight reaches the walker only through vertical cable components
(horizontal cables at pelvis height add zero wheel load — tested).

2.3 Arrest dynamics (1-D along the force): m s'' = m ω² s − F (ankle CoP at support edge, optimistic);
cable lumped stiffness 15 kN/m, damping ratio 0.2; brake slips at F_lim; walker Coulomb friction.
Passive-slip coverage (headline): for each caught trial, F_lim = the along-fall component of the force the group-tuned
passive brakes produce for that trial's fall direction (cables whose length grows along the motion slip at their own
tension, BWS slack; directions binned to {desc:R/dyn/passive_params/dir_res_deg:1} deg); the walker's friction load uses the full horizontal resultant
(along + perpendicular) and the downward cable component; the static tip check uses the statics of the actual slip
tensions (SF >= {desc:R/dyn/passive_params/SF:1} at the peak force); the dynamic tip check uses their overturning moment about the tip edge.
The fixed-force metric ({int:K/F_lim} N along the fall, QP tensions) is kept for comparison.
Success = peak s ≤ 0.25 m (SPEC fall criterion). Minimum constant force to stop:
F ≥ m ω(ω s0 + v); if s0 + v/ω > 0.25 m, F = m(ω²(0.25² − s0²) + v²) / (2(0.25 − s0)).

2.4 Dynamic tip check: rigid body rotating about the tipping edge under the arrest force history (indicative).

2.5 Spool/brake: brake torque = 1.5 × T × r_s, brake on the spool side of the gearbox. Nudge servo 12:1,
η = 0.9, rotor inertia 5e-6 kg·m² (assumed, not a part selection).

2.6 Simplifications that flatter the device: ankle CoP at support edge, fixed force direction, rigid frame,
nominal pelvis position, LIP patient; the perpendicular component of the passive slip force is applied to the walker
but not fed back into the 1-D patient dynamics. Stepping after engagement ignored (works against the device).

