## 6. NOT validated
No hardware or human data; no stress analysis (columns, base, anchors, belt, terminations, brake shock);
no spool inertia/backlash. "Required force" uses a 1-D LIP stopping model. μ, cable stiffness, walker CG/mass,
servo data are assumptions. Braked omni wheels can slip along roller axes → rubber outrigger pads credited,
not wheel brakes. Outrigger deployment time and front-anchor routing across the open U are not designed.
Independent slipping of several brakes will not preserve force direction (modelled for the walker, not fed back into
the patient dynamics). {lock28} s lock delay is the SPEC
value; 0.14 s is a guess. {desc:R/params/XI_ARREST:1} BW lift tolerance and belt loads unvalidated. No mobile cable system rated for
200–300 N arrest was found (mTPAD ≈35 N per cable).

## 7. Suggested wording
"We propose a walker-mounted cable interface with transparent slack, graded nudging (≤ {nudge_n} N) and a
power-off spool brake that slips at a set force (passive slip brakes give ≥ {int:K/F_lim} N along the fall direction and
≤ {int:K/resultant_bound_all_slip} N horizontal resultant). A load analysis shows arrest on a walker is
feasible only with an ~{int:K/mass} kg low-CG walker, deployable outriggers and mixed-height anchors; in the
simulated catches the passive slip brakes sufficed in {int:R/dyn/passive/280/k} of the {ncaught} trials that the CP simulation ended as CAUGHT
(lock {lock28} s, one seeded {ntot}-trial run; the other trials are unaffected). The concept is unbuilt and
unvalidated."

