## 8b. Front gate (hinged arm carrying the front-centre anchor; data/gate.json from gate_check.py)

**Design.** A horizontal arm at pelvis height (z = {geo:K/front_gate/hinge/2:2} m) is hinged on the front-right column at ({geo:K/front_gate/hinge/0:2}, {geo:K/front_gate/hinge/1:2}) and swings {int:K/front_gate/open_angle_deg} deg open toward the outside (+x) for entry and exit; closed, it spans the opening and latches onto the front-left column at ({geo:K/front_gate/latch/0:2}, +{geo:K/front_gate/latch/1:2}); hinge-to-latch distance {geo:K/front_gate/arm_length:2} m. The front-centre spool is mounted at the arm mid-span, so with the gate latched its cable exit is exactly the contract anchor ({geo:K/centre_anchors/1/0:2}, 0, {geo:K/centre_anchors/1/2:2}) and the analysis geometry (sections 3-8) is unchanged. The cable line of action meets the arm axis, so the arm sees no torsion. With the gate open nothing stands in the entry opening below pelvis height.

**Interlock.** A latch sensor reports gate latched / not latched. The controller refuses to arm (no nudge, no catch) unless the gate is latched, and drops to safe-stop if the latch opens while armed. The mechanical latch is rated for at least {req:K/front_gate/design_loads_N/latch_rating_min:1} N (1.5 x the design cable force); a failure of the latch while armed (cantilever case) is therefore out of scope.

**Design loads on the gate** (the front-centre cable force, horizontal along x): (a) front-centre passive slip tension {req:K/front_gate/design_loads_N/T_slip_centre:1} N; (b) worst controller (QP) tension on frontC over the arrest and nudge rows of data/results.json: {req:G/loads/T_qp_frontC_worst:1} N (arrest {req:G/loads/T_qp_frontC_arrest:1} N, nudge {req:G/loads/T_qp_frontC_nudge:1} N). The gate is sized for the larger, {req:G/loads/design_cable_force:1} N.

